.TH KRYLOV 2
.SH NAME
krylov: new, cmd, solve \- matrix-free GMRES and conjugate-gradient solvers
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "krylov.m";
krylov := load Krylov Krylov->PATH;
Solver: import krylov;

s := krylov->new();
s.cmd("method gmres\enrestart 30\entolerance 1e-8\enmaxiter 200");
x := s.solve(apply, b, nil);
if(!s.converged)
	...
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Krylov
solves
.IB apply( x ) " = " b
for
.I x
by GMRES(m) with restart or by conjugate gradient (CG), configured by a
small command language in the same style as
.IR plot (2)'s
own
.BR cmd .
.I Apply
is a plain function reference \(em
.B "fn(x: array of real): array of real"
\(em never a stored matrix: the only workable shape for anything built
on a grid, where even a modest mesh makes the dense matrix far too large
to assemble or store.
.PP
It must return a
.I fresh
array each call rather than a reused buffer.
The solver updates the residual in place against the array it is given
back, so a shared buffer would be corrupted between iterations.
Every implementation in this tree \(em
.IR sparse (2)'s
.BR matvec ,
.IR pde (2),
.IR fem (2)
and
.IR gpu (2)
\(em already allocates one per call.
.PP
.B New
returns a
.B Solver
defaulted to GMRES, tolerance
.IR 1e\-8 ,
restart
.IR 30 ,
and
.IR 200
maximum iterations.
.B Cmd
accepts one or more newline-separated commands:
.TP
.BI "method " gmres|cg
Selects the algorithm.
.B Gmres
is the general-purpose default: valid for any nonsingular operator,
symmetric or not.
.B Cg
does half the work per iteration and needs no restart or basis storage,
but is only mathematically valid when the operator is genuinely
symmetric positive definite \(em handed one that isn't, it can fail
silently or diverge.  Whether an operator qualifies is a real property
of the equation being solved
.RI ( pde (2)
notes which of its own equations do), not a style preference.
.TP
.BI "tolerance " t
Relative residual tolerance.
.TP
.BI "restart " k
GMRES restart length; ignored by CG.
.TP
.BI "maxiter " n
Iteration cap.
.PP
.B Solve
runs the configured method from
.I x0
(a zero vector if
.I x0
is
.BR nil ),
and returns the solution.  It also sets, for the caller to inspect:
.B iterations
(how many were taken),
.B residual
(the final relative residual norm), and
.B converged
(whether
.B residual
met
.BR tolerance ) \(em a caller can then decide whether to trust the
result, retry with a tighter setup, or report failure, rather than
silently using a possibly-garbage answer.
.SH SOURCE
.B /module/krylov.m
.br
.B /appl/lib/krylov.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR pde (2),
.IR plot (2)
